Introduction:Phishing is a form of cyber attack where criminals pose as legitimate individuals/organisations in order to trick users into divulging confidential information, such as passwords, banking or account details.
Common Warning Signs of Phishing:Phishing attack will often have tell-tale signs that you can look out for to identify phishing emails. The first being that the email will come from an unknown source or similar to one from a genuine organisation. You may also notice that they create a sense of urgency as they will try and tell you that your account will be suspended/locked unless acted upon straight away. Another tell-tale sign is suspicious links within the email that lead to fraudulent websites where your personal information can be stolen. Some phishing emails will also include unexpected attachments which could install malicious programmes. Finally attackers will often ask for highly confidential information such as passwords, verification codes and banking details. Identifying these tell-tale signs are important so you don’t fall victim to phishing attacks and protect your personal information and online accounts.
Practical example:A Botho University student receives an email from someone claiming their student portal account will be locked unless they verify their password within 30 minutes.The email includes a link which appears to be the login page of the university.
How to respond safely:Do not click on any links or download any attachments that you have any suspicions regarding. Check your message using official university communication channels.If you have entered your password into a fake website you should change your password on the real website as soon as possible. Enable Multi-Factor Authentication on this account and report this to the university IT department.
Conclusion:Your conclusion needs to explain how by knowing the signs of phishing, checking suspicious messages and reporting incidents will help reduce the risk of identity theft, malware and unauthorised access to accounts.
